skills/implement/scripts/test-step2-dispatch.md:25:10. Second invocation against a tmpdir whose `step2-spawn-coder.txt` recorded a different coder (`codex` pre-seeded; invocation passes `--coder=cursor --cursor-present true`) emits `STATUS=bailed REASON=coder-mismatch-tmpdir-reuse TOOL=cursor`. The pre-seeded sentinel value MUST be unchanged on bail, and the `cursor-resume-count.txt` MUST NOT have been written — pins the cross-coder guard's "fail before any per-tool state mutation" ordering. Also asserts `ORCHESTRATOR_EDIT_AUTHORITY=forbidden` on this bail path.
skills/implement/scripts/test-step2-dispatch.md:41:- Single-retry on transient launcher failure with clean-state guard.
skills/umbrella/scripts/helpers.md:19:Stdout grammar: `EDGES_ADDED=<N>`, per-edge `EDGE_<j>_BLOCKER=<N>` / `EDGE_<j>_BLOCKED=<M>`, `EDGES_REJECTED_CYCLE=<N>`, `EDGES_SKIPPED_EXISTING=<N>` (already-present edges, including idempotent 422 already-exists responses per `add-blocked-by.sh:193-196`), `EDGES_SKIPPED_API_UNAVAILABLE=<N>` (repo-wide probe failure OR per-edge feature-missing 404 — both mean "the GitHub dependency surface was not usable here for this run"; **semantics intentionally preserved** by issue #728's fix — see `PROBE_FAILED` below for the cause disambiguator), `EDGES_FAILED=<N>` covers two categories: (a) per-edge **operational** failures — rate-limit, permission denied, ambiguous 404, other 4xx, 5xx, request-shape mismatches, blocker-id lookup failure, network — failures that do NOT match the feature-missing fingerprint; (b) per-edge **policy-driven** failures — `bound-exhausted`, set when the transitive `blocked_by` traversal hit `WIRE_DAG_TRAVERSAL_NODE_CAP` and the per-edge cycle-check loop's `CYCLE=false` answer cannot be trusted on a known-incomplete TSV (issue #718 fail-closed posture, voted 3-0 in the design-phase dialectic). `PROBE_FAILED=<0|1>` (issue #728) is a parse-only per-run flag distinguishing the two repo-wide-skip causes: `0` = confirmed feature-missing (probe got a fingerprinted 404) **OR** no probe attempted (e.g., `--no-backlinks` first-child path with empty `CHILDREN_FILE` — `probe_target` was empty, no probe ran, `PROBE_FAILED` stays at its initialization value); `1` = transient/operational probe failure (5xx + retry also failed, or HTTP response other than 2xx/fingerprinted-404). The retry policy is one network-only retry on `5xx` and empty-status (no HTTP response at all); `429`, other `4xx`, and `2xx`/fingerprinted-404 are single-attempt (clear HTTP responses are not transport blips). `BACKLINKS_POSTED=<N>`, `BACKLINKS_SKIPPED_EXISTING=<N>` (count of children whose existing back-link comment matching <code>Part of umbrella #${UMBRELLA} — </code> was detected in the comment stream; renamed from `BACKLINKS_SKIPPED_NATIVE` per #716 — see semantic migration note below).
skills/umbrella/scripts/helpers.md:32:**Semantic migration note (issue #716)**: Before this change, the back-link loop probed the child's `blocked_by` list for the umbrella issue number — intended to detect GitHub's native umbrella-rendering surface — and incremented `BACKLINKS_SKIPPED_NATIVE` on a hit. The probe was unreachable because no path in `/umbrella` ever created the umbrella↔child native edge in the direction that probe tested (parent-of/child-of vs blocker-of), so `BACKLINKS_SKIPPED_NATIVE` was always 0 and re-runs accumulated duplicate `Part of umbrella #M — ...` comments on each child. The check now scans the child's existing comment stream for the literal prefix <code>Part of umbrella #${UMBRELLA} — </code> (the exact prefix the tool itself emits, with the trailing ` — ` separator preventing prefix-collision on numeric umbrella numbers — e.g., `#1` would otherwise false-match `#12`). The counter is renamed `BACKLINKS_SKIPPED_NATIVE` → `BACKLINKS_SKIPPED_EXISTING` to reflect the new semantics. The counter is in the parse-only set (not propagated through `output.kv`), so this is an internal-contract rename — no change to the orchestrator's `output.kv` shape. Dashboards or playbooks parsing wire-dag stdout for the old name will need a one-line update; operators will see a non-zero `BACKLINKS_SKIPPED_EXISTING` on re-runs whose children already have a back-link comment, where the old `BACKLINKS_SKIPPED_NATIVE` always read 0.
skills/umbrella/scripts/helpers.md:52:`--dry-run`: emit `TITLES_RENAMED=0`, `TITLES_SKIPPED_EXISTING=0`, `TITLES_FAILED=0`, exit 0. No `gh issue edit` calls. Provided for direct callers / regression tests / future callers that want to short-circuit the rename loop without a separate gate; the orchestrator path through SKILL.md Step 3B.4 itself does NOT exercise this flag — Step 3B.4 is fully skipped when `DRY_RUN=true` (no `wire-dag`, no `prefix-titles`), so the helper only sees a real-children invocation in production.
skills/umbrella/scripts/helpers.md:56:`prefix-titles` stdout keys are **parse-only** — `TITLES_RENAMED` / `TITLES_SKIPPED_EXISTING` / `TITLES_FAILED` are not propagated through `output.kv` (consistent with `wire-dag`'s `EDGES_REJECTED_CYCLE` / `EDGES_SKIPPED_*` / `BACKLINKS_SKIPPED_EXISTING` precedent — internal coordination signals, not part of the canonical Step 4 grammar). Changes to the parse-only stdout grammar require a same-PR update to `SKILL.md` Step 3B.4 plus this paragraph and the stdout grammar above. A future decision to surface rename counters in `output.kv` would require updating Step 4 of `SKILL.md` and the canonical KV-grammar block in this file's edit-in-sync rules below.
skills/umbrella/scripts/helpers.md:64:Changes to `wire-dag` stdout keys consumed by orchestrator emit-output (i.e., keys that flow through `output.kv` per SKILL.md Step 4) require a same-PR update to `SKILL.md` Step 4 AND Step 3B.4. New `wire-dag` stdout keys consumed only for orchestrator parsing / session state (NOT propagated through `output.kv`) require updates only to `SKILL.md` Step 3B.4 + this file's stdout-grammar paragraph above. Today the `output.kv`-propagated set is `EDGES_ADDED`, `EDGE_<j>_BLOCKER`, `EDGE_<j>_BLOCKED`, `BACKLINKS_POSTED`; the parse-only set is `EDGES_REJECTED_CYCLE`, `EDGES_SKIPPED_EXISTING`, `EDGES_SKIPPED_API_UNAVAILABLE`, `EDGES_FAILED`, `PROBE_FAILED`, `BACKLINKS_SKIPPED_EXISTING`.
skills/umbrella/scripts/test-render-umbrella-body.md:3:**Purpose**: runtime conformance harness for `render-umbrella-body.sh`. Closes #645 — the script previously emitted success KVs (`UMBRELLA_BODY_FILE=` / `UMBRELLA_TITLE_HINT=`) on stdout in some failure-case paths, masking failure as success in `/umbrella` Step 3B.3. After the fix, the script: (1) rejects non-writable `--tmpdir` upfront with the documented `ERROR=tmpdir not writable: <path>` stderr line; (2) writes the body via mktemp partial → `[ -s ]` verify → `mv` atomic rename, emitting success KVs only past the `mv` gate. This harness pins those invariants.
skills/umbrella/scripts/test-render-umbrella-body.md:9:3. **`mv` failure** via PATH-injected fake `mv` that always exits 1. Asserts: exit non-zero, stderr contains `ERROR=failed to write umbrella body:`, stdout contains NEITHER success KV. This case exercises the new checked-write branch's `mv` guard. PATH-injection is used because BSD `mv` (macOS) and GNU `mv` differ on what makes a same-directory rename fail (e.g., dest-as-empty-dir succeeds on BSD by moving source inside, fails with EISDIR on GNU); mocking `mv` removes that platform divergence and isolates the test to the script's own error-handling logic.
skills/umbrella/scripts/test-render-umbrella-body.md:10:3b. **Pre-existing dest as directory** — `mkdir -p $TMPDIR/umbrella-body.md`. Exercises the pre-rename `[ -e "$OUT" ] && [ ! -f "$OUT" ]` guard added in response to the BSD `mv source dir/` silent-nesting bug (same #645 failure-as-success class on a different surface — Codex caught this during code review). Asserts: exit non-zero, stderr `ERROR=failed to write umbrella body:`, no success KVs.
skills/umbrella/scripts/test-umbrella-blocked-by-issue.md:9:**Edit-in-sync rules**: any change to the literals asserted by this harness requires a same-PR update to `skills/umbrella/SKILL.md`, and any change to the corresponding `SKILL.md` contract requires updating this harness. Keep the scoped block boundaries aligned with the Step 3A / 3B.2 / 3B.3 anchors/headings if those anchors or headings are renamed.
skills/implement/scripts/hook-post-bump-version.md:5:When `$IMPLEMENT_TMPDIR/.bump-version-armed` exists (written by `check-bump-version.sh --mode pre` inside the Rebase + Re-bump Sub-procedure step 4) AND `$IMPLEMENT_TMPDIR/postbump-state.sh` does NOT yet exist (i.e., the orchestrator has not finished the post-bump continuation), the hook emits a continuation `➡️` directive into `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`. The directive tells the orchestrator that `APPLIED=true COMMIT_SHA=<sha>` is NOT a run-completion signal and that `check-bump-version.sh --mode post --before-count $COMMITS_BEFORE` is the FIRST permitted next Bash tool call. The hook is a mechanical backstop for issue #2338, parallel to the directive-injection role of `hook-post-design.sh` for the post-`/design` boundary; the Stop-hook block in `hook-stop-fail-close.sh` remains the session-stop safety net.
skills/implement/scripts/hook-post-bump-version.md:7:Stdout is either empty or a JSON object with `hookSpecificOutput.hookEventName="PostToolUse"` and `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` carrying the directive. Exit code is always 0; non-`bump-version` Skill calls, missing `jq`, malformed stdin, no qualifying tmpdir, missing `.bump-version-armed` sentinel, or already-written `postbump-state.sh` are fail-open no-ops. The hook does NOT write or clear any sentinel file — its only side effect is the directive emission.
skills/implement/scripts/hook-post-bump-version.md:9:Edit in sync with `lib-resolve-implement-tmpdir.sh`, `hook-stop-fail-close.sh` (the paired session-stop block keyed on the same `.bump-version-armed` / `postbump-state.sh` sentinels), `hooks/hooks.json` (registration entry), `skills/implement/SKILL.md` (NEVER #15), `skills/implement/references/rebase-rebump-subprocedure.md` (step 4 "Continue after child returns" anti-halt reminder), and `scripts/test-implement-anti-halt.sh` (regression coverage).
skills/implement/scripts/step2-implement.md:9:- Spawn-time baseline files are written ONCE on the first invocation under `$TMPDIR_ARG`: `step2-baseline.txt` (HEAD SHA), `step2-spawn-branch.txt` (branch name), `step2-plugin-json-baseline.txt` (`git hash-object` of `.claude-plugin/plugin.json`). All resume invocations reuse them. The baseline SHA anchors the launcher-retry "clean state" guard (post-failure HEAD must equal baseline). The baseline-vs-HEAD diff cross-check that previously enforced manifest path-set equality was removed when the dispatcher took over committing — there is no longer a committed Codex diff to compare against.
skills/implement/scripts/step2-implement.md:12:- Immediately after canonicalizing `$TMPDIR_ARG`, the dispatcher exports `IMPLEMENT_TMPDIR="$TMPDIR_ARG"`. If `$TMPDIR_ARG/session-id` exists and is non-empty, its trimmed contents overwrite any inherited `LARCH_TOKEN_SESSION_ID`; the canonical tmpdir file wins over stale environment state on external-implementer paths. If `$TMPDIR_ARG/claude-source.env` exists, `LARCH_CLAUDE_SOURCE_FILE` is exported to that snapshot path. The leaf launchers repeat the same overwrite defensively before spawning external tools.
skills/implement/scripts/step2-implement.md:15:- Launcher wrapper exit is captured separately from the implementer-reported `LAUNCHER_EXIT=` KV. Wrapper exit `2` is a validation failure and emits `STATUS=bailed REASON=wrapper-validation-failure` immediately without retrying. Wrapper exit `0` keeps the existing KV parsing behavior. Other non-zero wrapper exits enter the existing one-shot retry path only when no manifest was written and post-failure state is fully clean (`git status --porcelain` empty, no `.git/index.lock`, HEAD == `BASELINE_SHA`). Launcher stdout/stderr is captured to a temp file under the canonical `$TMPDIR_ARG`, capped to 65 KiB for bail-path parsing, and removed by an EXIT trap.
skills/implement/scripts/step2-implement.md:16:- The dispatcher does NOT `git reset`, NOT `git checkout`, NOT discard working-tree state. On `status=complete` it stages and commits implementer edits via `git add -A && git commit -F <commit-message-file>` — `commit_message` is taken from the manifest with no diff or subject cross-check, but IS piped through `scripts/redact-secrets.sh` immediately before `git commit -F` so the secrets-family scrubber that protects the canonical on-disk manifest also protects git history. After the successful dispatcher commit, it invokes `scripts/larch-log-flush.sh` best-effort so active `/implement` log writes are flushed behind the implementer commit. On any other status, the dispatcher leaves the working tree untouched. On `commit-failed`, `git add -A` has already run and the index stays staged — operators inspect `git status` and `$IMPLEMENT_TMPDIR/${TOOL_TAG}-commit-stderr.txt` (where the failed `git commit` stderr is captured) before deciding whether to `git reset` or amend. Implementer hard guard #1 (no destructive git ops) is mirrored here as "the dispatcher never destroys operator work either."
