skills/umbrella/scripts/render-batch-input.md:26:2. **`### OOS_<N>:` false positive**: `parse-input.sh` runs OOS-heading detection (regex `^\#\#\#[[:space:]]+OOS_[0-9]+:[[:space:]]+(.+)$`) BEFORE the plain `^\#\#\#[[:space:]]+(.+)$` branch. The OOS branch's #132 guard at `parse-input.sh:381-382` ABSORBS an `### OOS_<N>: …` line into an active generic body (`CURRENT_MODE=="generic"` AND `IN_BODY==true` AND non-empty `CURRENT_BODY`) instead of splitting. The case-(f) guard rejects bodies containing such lines anyway, since `### OOS_<N>: …` content inside an `/umbrella` piece body is itself anomalous (piece bodies are implementation contracts, not OOS-list scaffolding). Accepted trade-off: a parser-aware case-(f) guard that mirrored the OOS-then-plain ordering would couple `render-batch-input.sh` to multiple `parse-input.sh` regexes plus the pending-heading state — heavy for a renderer's input validator.
skills/umbrella/scripts/render-batch-input.md:30:**Piece-body item-split hazard (#831)**: each piece body in the rendered batch markdown is concatenated after a `### <piece title>` line and flows verbatim into `/issue --input-file`'s parser at `skills/issue/scripts/parse-input.sh`. That parser is line-based and, in **generic mode** (where `/umbrella`'s pieces always land — the surrounding `### <piece title>` heading itself triggers generic-mode entry via Path 3), treats any `^### <title>` line in a piece body as a new-item boundary (Path 3 at `parse-input.sh:393-423`: `flush_item` + start a new generic item). Result: the piece silently splits into multiple parsed items with wrong titles and broken `depends_on` index alignment relative to the original `pieces.json`. Piece bodies (the `body` field of each `pieces.json` entry rendered on this normal-mode pipeline) MUST NOT contain line-start `^### <title>` lines, including inside fenced code blocks (the parser does not track fence state). The producer-side rule is enforced at the prompt boundary in `skills/umbrella/SKILL.md` Step 3B.1's `body` bullet; the consumer-side catch-all (covering operator-prebuilt batches that bypass Step 3B.1 AND this renderer — `umbrella --input-file` / `issue --input-file` direct — plus other upstream producers) lives in `skills/issue/scripts/parse-input.md` under "Reverse coupling: `/umbrella`'s piece bodies (#831)". Scope of THIS contract is the **normal-mode pipeline only**: pre-decomposed-input mode skips both `/umbrella` Step 3B.1 and this renderer entirely, so the producer-side prompt directive does not run for those callers. There is no body-line escape mechanism in `render-batch-input.sh` analogous to `/research`'s `\\### …` defense (see `skills/research/scripts/render-findings-batch.md` "Body composition"); protection here is contract-level only.
skills/implement/scripts/refresh-execution-issues.md:12:All session state is read from files under `IMPLEMENT_TMPDIR` rather than
skills/research/scripts/render-findings-batch.md:60:The slicer is **fence-aware**: lines matching `^[[:space:]]*` followed by three backticks toggle an `IN_FENCE` boolean. Indented fences (e.g., a 3-space-prefixed fenced block inside a bulleted item's body) toggle the state correctly per #510 review FINDING_5 — earlier drafts matched only column-0 fences and would misparse indented fenced contents. Header detection is suppressed inside fenced blocks (defense against `### Foo` inside an inline code block). See FINDING_5b.
skills/research/references/eval-set.md:100:- **question**: What concurrency hazards exist when `/fix-issue` and `/implement` run against the same tracking issue, and how does the IN PROGRESS comment lock interact with the `parent-issue.md` sentinel?
skills/research/references/eval-set.md:103:- **expected_keywords**: IN PROGRESS, find-lock-issue.sh, single-runner, sentinel, ADOPTED
